.venv/
out/
data/*
!data/distill.jsonl
__pycache__/
*.pyc
wandb/
# suite v1 payload is regenerated byte-identically from committed seeds
# (44 MB, 3.3 s); the per-phase COMMITMENTS are committed so the regeneration is
# verifiable without shipping the data.
#
# This block is deny-by-default on purpose. It used to name only `manifest.json`
# and `SHA256SUMS` -- the RETIRED whole-suite commitment that
# agentlab.suite.generate.LEGACY_COMMITMENTS now refuses on sight -- and the
# live per-phase files were neither named nor ignored: they survived by accident,
# because `data/*` does not descend into `data/suite/v1/`. The train/dev pair
# additionally survived by already being tracked. That accident is not a
# protection: it evaporates the first time anyone writes `data/**` or
# `data/suite/v1/*` above this block, and it would have taken the held-out
# release out of R silently. So: ignore everything under the suite directory,
# then name the five files a commitment is allowed to consist of.
!data/suite/
data/suite/*
!data/suite/v1/
data/suite/v1/*
# train/dev phase -- committed at P, from the seeds in configs/suite_v1.toml
!data/suite/v1/manifest.train-dev.json
!data/suite/v1/SHA256SUMS.train-dev
# held-out phase -- committed at R, and only at R: its seeds derive from L, so
# these two files cannot exist before the lock. R must carry both; a reveal
# without its manifest lets the evaluated bytes be chosen after the fact.
!data/suite/v1/manifest.heldout.json
!data/suite/v1/SHA256SUMS.heldout
# the one extra path the reveal state machine is allowed to add at R
!data/suite/v1/suite_release.json
# the payload itself (specs/kb/oracles/certspecs) stays out, and so does the
# retired whole-suite commitment: `data/suite/v1/*` already covers both, and
# these lines say so out loud rather than leaving it to be re-derived.
data/suite/v1/specs/
data/suite/v1/kb/
data/suite/v1/oracles/
data/suite/v1/certspecs/
data/suite/v1/manifest.json
data/suite/v1/SHA256SUMS
